.TH SVCD 8
.SH NAME
svcd \- run and restart services on a headless Inferno host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svcd
[
.B -c
.I config
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Svcd
is the service manager for a command-line Inferno machine.
It reads a list of services, runs each one as its own process group in
its own namespace, restarts it when it exits, and records status under
.BR /tmp/svcd .
.PP
The configuration is read from
.B /lib/svcd.conf
or, when it exists, from
.BR /n/host/svcd.conf .
The
.B -c
option names another file.
Each non-comment line is a service name followed by the command and its
arguments:
.IP
.EX
# name	command args...
cpu	/dis/sh.dis -c 'listen -s tcp!*!rstyx {runas $user cpud &}'
tick	/dis/sh.dis -c 'echo tick; /dis/sleep.dis 3'
-crash	/dis/willfail.dis
.EE
.PP
A leading
.B -
disables a service without removing the line.
The command line is split with
.IR string (2)
quoting rules, so
.B '...'
groups arguments containing spaces.
.PP
Each service is started with
.BR pctl (2)
.BR NEWPGRP ,
.BR FORKNS
and
.BR FORKFD :
it gets a private copy of the namespace, its own file descriptors, and
a new process group.  Its standard input, output and error are
redirected to
.BR /tmp/svcd/\fIname\fP.log ,
which is truncated on each start.
.PP
Because the service has its own process group, a service that
daemonises is still supervised.
.I Listen
does exactly that: its
.I init
spawns the listener and returns.
.I Svcd
notices that the group is still alive and keeps supervising it instead
of restarting into
.IR "address in use" .
Use
.B listen -s
if the service should stay in the foreground.
.PP
When a service exits, it is restarted with an exponential backoff of
1, 2, 4, 8, 16 and then 30 seconds.  A run that lasted more than 30
seconds resets the backoff to one second, so a service that starts
cleanly and runs for a while is restarted immediately after a crash.
.SS Control
.I Svcd
polls
.B /tmp/svcd/ctl
once a second.  Write one command per line:
.IP
.EX
start \fIname\fP
stop \fIname\fP
restart \fIname\fP
stopall
startall
status
.EE
.PP
.I Svcctl
is the convenient interface.
.SH FILES
.TF /tmp/svcd/status
.TP
.B /lib/svcd.conf
service list (default)
.TP
.B /n/host/svcd.conf
host-provided service list, preferred when present
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/status
one line per service:
.BI name " " state " pid=" pid " restarts=" count
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/ctl
control file
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/log
.I svcd
events: starts, exits, backoff, control commands
.TP
.BI /tmp/svcd/ name .log
service standard output and error
.TP
.B /tmp/svcd/svcd.pid
.I svcd
process id
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
; cat /lib/svcd.conf
cpu	/dis/sh.dis -c 'listen -s tcp!*!rstyx /dis/cpud.dis'
; svcd &
; svcctl
cpu running pid=31 restarts=0
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/svcd.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR svcctl (1),
.IR listen (1),
.IR cpud (8),
.IR svc (8)
.SH BUGS
Supervision is liveness of a pid or of a live process group, not of
the service's health: a wedged listener is left alone.  There is no
per-service resource limit, user switch, dependency ordering, or idle
timeout.  Status is written when state changes, not continuously.
.PP
Because the control file is read whole and truncated, two commands
written in the same second run as one; use
.I svcctl
for anything scripted.
